# --- FIRMWARE: explicit keep-list -------------------------------------------
# auto-config.sh now passes `--firmware-chroot false`. Without it, live-build's
# chroot_firmware step runs Firmware_List_From_Contents(), which downloads the
# mirror's Contents-*.gz and installs EVERY package owning a file under
# lib/firmware/ -- see /usr/share/live/build/functions/firmwarelists.sh:53.
# That path ignores the dependency graph entirely, which is why the v0.1.1 image
# shipped 37 firmware/microcode packages when this list only ever asked for 5,
# and why 9 of them (firmware-ast, -b43-installer, -b43legacy-installer,
# -cirrus, -ivtv, -libertas, -siano, -zd1211, -nvidia-tesla-535-gsp) have no
# reverse dependency anywhere in Debian.
# LB_FIRMWARE_CHROOT defaults to true:
#   /usr/share/live/build/functions/configuration.sh:488
#
# Turning the firehose off leaves the 15 packages reachable from the five
# entries above (Depends+Recommends closure, verified against trixie's Packages
# index). Six of the 22 it drops are things a real laptop needs, so they are
# listed explicitly here instead of being left to chance. Everything else in the
# drop set is datacentre or legacy-capture hardware: netronome 140.6 MiB,
# nvidia-tesla-535-gsp 59.4, marvell-prestera 59.0, cavium 5.2, qlogic 4.6,
# myricom 3.9, bnx2x 2.3, netxen 2.1, ipw2x00 1.5, ivtv 1.2, bnx2 0.6, siano
# 1.3, zd1211 0.1, ast 0.04, b43-installer, b43legacy-installer.
# Net installed saving: 281.8 MiB.
#
# WHY THOSE ARE NOT LISTED EXPLICITLY: 0200-build-moko-shell.hook.chroot ends
# with `apt-get autoremove -y`, and these ten arrive as auto-installed. That
# looked like a trap -- firmware-intel-graphics is only a *Recommends* of
# firmware-misc-nonfree, so it is not protected by a Depends edge. Measured in a
# trixie container rather than assumed: autoremove removed 0 packages and all ten
# survived, because APT::AutoRemove::RecommendsImportant defaults to true and a
# Recommends edge from a manually-installed package therefore does protect.
# live-build adds no --no-install-recommends (APT_OPTIONS is literally
# "--yes -o Acquire::Retries=5"), so the Recommends really are installed.
# The presence assertions in scripts/test-iso-docker.sh are what guard this: if
# a future Debian release drops one of those Recommends, the test fails loudly
# instead of the image silently losing i915 firmware.
#
# Kept implicitly by the closure above, listed here for reference only (do not
# remove the comment, do not duplicate the entries):
#   firmware-linux-free, firmware-linux-nonfree, firmware-intel-graphics (i915),
#   firmware-intel-misc, firmware-mediatek, firmware-nvidia-graphics,
#   firmware-ath9k-htc, firmware-carl9170, amd64-microcode, intel-microcode
firmware-atheros
firmware-realtek
firmware-sof-signed
firmware-intel-sound
firmware-cirrus
firmware-libertas
